%I A398872 #25 Aug 26 2026 22:35:45
%S A398872 6,8,9,12,12,14,15,15,16,18,20,18,24,20,21,21,28,24,30,24,24,30,35,27,
%T A398872 30,28,27,30,42,32,45,30,30,42,32,32,60,36,33,33,56,38,60,35,36,42,63,
%U A398872 36,42,40,39,42,70,40,39,39,42,66,77,42,96,52,44,42,42,46,90,44,48,48
%N A398872 a(n) is the least x + y + z such that (x*y*z) / (x + y + z) = n, for positive integers x,y,z.
%C A398872 The two variable case of this is less interesting, and seems to be the sequence a(n) = 4 * n (A008586) based on empirical observation.
%H A398872 Hugo Pfoertner, <a href="/A398872/b398872.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A398872 a(n) >= 3 * sqrt(3 * n). If x + y + z = q for some constant q < 3 * sqrt(3 * n), then (x * y * z) / (x + y + z) = n if and only if x * y * z = qn. Given the fact that x + y + z = q for some constant q, x * y * z is maximized when x = y = z. So since q < 3 * sqrt(3 * n), x, y, z < sqrt(3 * n), but then (x * y * z) / (x + y + z) < (3n * sqrt(3 * n)) / (3 * sqrt(3 * n)) = n, so x, y, and z are not a viable solution. Thus, there exists no viable solutions when x + y + z < 3 * sqrt(3 * n).
%F A398872 A corollary of this is that when sqrt(3 * n) is an integer, then a(n) = 3 * sqrt(3 * n). In this case, when x = y = z = sqrt(3 * n), (x * y * z) / (x + y + z) = (3n * sqrt(3 * n)) / (3 * sqrt(3 * n)) = n.
%F A398872 a(n) <= 2n + 4, since for all n, if x = 2, y = n, and z = n + 2, (x * y * z) / (x + y + z) = (2 * n * (n + 2)) / (2 + n + (n + 2)) = n, and x + y + z = 2 + n + (n + 2) = 2n + 4. So there always exists a valid solution where x + y + z = 2n + 4. This bound is exactly achieved when n = 1, 2, and 4.
%e A398872 If x = 3, y = 4, and z = 7, then (x * y * z) / (x + y + z) = 84 / 14 = 6. We can check that x + y + z = 14 is less than (or at least as good as) any other valid solution. So, a(6) = 14.
%t A398872 a[n_] := Minimize[x + y + z, x*y*z/(x + y + z) == n && x > 0 && y >= x && z >= y, {x, y, z}, Integers][[1]] (* _Hugo Pfoertner_, Aug 20 2026 *)
%o A398872 (Python)
%o A398872 import math
%o A398872 def a(i):
%o A398872     q = math.isqrt(27 * i)
%o A398872     while True:
%o A398872         for a in range(1, q + 1):
%o A398872             for b in range(a, q + 1 - a):
%o A398872                 c = q - a - b
%o A398872                 if c >= b:
%o A398872                     if (a * b * c) / (a + b + c) == i:
%o A398872                         return q
%o A398872         q = q + 1
%o A398872 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 71)])
%o A398872 (PARI) a(n) = for(m=1, oo, for(x=1, m\3, for(y=x, (m-x)\2, my(z=m-x-y); if(x*y*z == m*n, return(m)) ))) \\ _Andrew Howroyd_, Aug 11 2026
%K A398872 nonn,easy,new
